{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019XEUG1XSMvBLzsRQB2etVo","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Nightly Sweep System — Execution Plan (v4)\n2\t**Project codename:** *Aurora* (Always-on Understanding of Research, Operations, and Reasoning Agents)\n3\t**Author:** drafted with John · v1 2026-05-15 (initial) · v2 2026-05-15 (post deep-think/deep-research review) · v3 2026-05-15 (decisions locked in §17) · v4 2026-05-18 (post-launch reconciliation: §18 status, §19 GWL/OpenClaw integration scope, §20 next-steps punch list) · **v4.1 2026-05-18 PM (audit-driven reconciliation: 4 parallel audit subagents verified every §18 + §18.3 claim against the live install; §18.2 extended with 13 new gaps the doc had missed, §18.3 corrected on the Gemini-CLI install false claim, §20 fully re-prioritized into a 22-item plan; §21 audit stamp added)** · **v4.2 2026-05-19 (overnight delta in §18.2.quater: skills+tools sweeps ok, agents sweep hard-failed on Anthropic 529 Overloaded; KB to 356 artifacts; CHANGELOG total-drift widened; publisher slot drifted to 08:30; Bucket A-D items resolved or partially-resolved are stamped in §20)** · **v4.3 2026-05-19 PM (deep-review-driven next-steps plan locked into §22; §18.6 captures today's BOM-fix → plugin install + Bucket B #5/#6 verification + voyage-style next-steps proposal from 4 parallel deep-think/deep-research/expert-coder/expert-coder-win11 agents; cuts and re-gates marked in §20; first task: Bucket F1 git-init aurora source)**\n4\t**Status:** Deployed and self-running. Nightly sweeps + publisher + morning briefing all firing on schedule via Windows Task Scheduler (verified: 5/17→5/18 sweeps at 05:00/06:00/07:00 UTC on time). KB at 335 artifacts, MTD spend $0.74/$60 caps. Gaps documented in §18 (extended in v4.1 to 22 items); integration work scoped in §19; re-prioritized punch list in §20; audit stamp in §21.\n5\t\n6\t**Change log from v1 → v2**\n7\t- Applied the `deep-think` and `deep-research` skill protocols explicitly (see §0).\n8\t- Added **Templates** as a 4th artifact class caught by the Skills sweep (config templates, prompt templates, project scaffolds, document templates, agent templates like OpenClaw SOUL.md configs). Tagged with `kind='template'` in the schema; lighter-weight validation (no runner exec — they're not directly runnable).\n9\t- Promoted the **dual-axis** framing in §7 to be explicit: *what it does* × *how complex a task it handles*. Both axes apply uniformly across all four artifact kinds.\n10\t- Added §15 — **Helping Hand**, a meta-skill that runs in any Cowork/Claude Code session and recommends from the KB. Default mode: approve-then-load. Skill + MCP server packaged together as `helping-hand.plugin`.\n11\t- Added §16 — **GitHub collaborator distribution**. Single private repo. Publisher stage commits scrubbed `kb-snapshot.sqlite` (no transcripts), full markdown library, and the latest `helping-hand.plugin` after each nightly cycle. Daily `YYYYMMDD` tags.\n12\t\n13\t**Change log v2 → v3**\n14\t- All 10 §17 open questions resolved; decisions are committed and visible in §17 (renamed \"Decisions logged\").\n15\t- Added §9.4 — **08:30 morning briefing** as a Cowork scheduled session that consolidates the three exec summaries, surfaces budget, and renders PAT expiry reminders.\n16\t- Added **budget tracking** end-to-end: `costs` table in §6 schema, one-line budget block in every exec summary, full breakdown + 7-day trend + cap-projection warnings in the morning briefing. Dedicated Aurora-scoped Anthropic + Gemini keys with monthly caps ($50 / $10 to start).\n17\t- Added **PAT expiry surfacing** in the morning briefing (7/3/1 days); publisher hard-fails-loud on 401 with a `PAT_EXPIRED.txt` in the workspace folder.\n18\t- Set Aurora root to **`C:\\dev\\aurora\\`** (top-level, separate from this planning folder).\n19\t- Docker locked as the runner sandbox; Windows Task Scheduler locked as primary scheduler; Gemini locked as Critic B primary with a configurable local-LLM fallback.\n20\t\n21\t**Change log v3 → v4 (post-launch reconciliation, 2026-05-18)**\n22\t- Added §18 (post-deployment status) — what's actually working vs. partial vs. gapped, including the overnight-outage incident and the hardening pass that followed.\n23\t- Added §19 (Ghost World Labs / OpenClaw integration scope) — non-executing sketch of how Helping Hand drops into GWL's GBrain-resolved OpenClaw agent stack. To be executed in a Cowork session opened inside `C:\\dev\\Agentic-Home`, not from this curator project.\n24\t- Added §20 (next-steps punch list) — 14 items prioritized by impact × effort across this-week / 1-2-weeks / later. Includes sunburst, neural_plasticity populate, template-detection heuristic, dedicated keys, GWL integration, threshold calibration, P9 trigger.\n25\t- Documented hardening pass that wasn't in the v3 plan: publisher logging, launcher logging, missed-run watchdog, hybrid YAML parser, WakeToRun + StartWhenAvailable, Active Hours + 03:00 reboot pin, cross-runtime HH install, collaborator distribution.\n26\t- Factual corrections: Hermes star count (~95K, not 140K), GBrain storage (Postgres+pgvector with MCP *interface*, not \"MCP-based memory\"), OpenClaw governance (steering committee, not Steinberger).\n27\t- Added prompt-injection quarantine stage between scout and screener.\n28\t- Made critic independence structural, not aspirational — different input streams + at least one cross-vendor critic.\n29\t- GitHub dedupe rewritten around canonical normalization + aliases table.\n30\t- Reproducibility: per-run snapshots of seed maps, prompt templates, model IDs.\n31\t- Honest framing on the hyperbolic map (v1 = sunburst; learned-hyperbolic only at ≥500 artifacts).\n32\t- Sweep queueing with a global lock file.\n33\t- Interestingness decay added.\n34\t- Clone-don't-import isolation for license/legal hygiene.\n35\t- Seed maps expanded with verified additions (Letta v1, Mem0, MemoryAgentBench, official MCP Registry, Smithery, MCPfinder, awesome-claude-skills, claude-plugins.dev, Anthropic memory tool docs, Anthropic \"dreaming\" preview, Simon Willison's annotated system prompts).\n36\t- Neural-plasticity schema expanded with credit_assignment_latency, update_reversibility, interference_scope, memory_write_permissions, shipped_eval_harness.\n37\t- Velikov seed dropped (off-theme; bookmarked separately as methodology-watch).\n38\t- Top-K is now budget-derived, not a constant.\n39\t- Added prompt-state diff for in-prompt learners.\n40\t\n41\t---\n42\t\n43\t## §0 — Protocol applications (auditable)\n44\t\n45\t### 0.1 Deep-Think protocol applied (per `C:\\dev\\.claude\\skills\\deep-think\\SKILL.md`)\n46\t\n47\t**Deconstruction.** The plan in v1 made these load-bearing claims:\n48\t- C1: Scouts can be parallelized safely against the open web.\n49\t- C2: A two-critic + reconciler swarm produces independent judgments.\n50\t- C3: URL+content-hash dedupe is sufficient.\n51\t- C4: A hand-defined taxonomy + hashed-sibling-angle Poincaré disk gives a meaningful hyperbolic embedding.\n52\t- C5: File-diff + DB-row inspection verifies \"what is modified\" claims for learning agents.\n53\t- C6: Scouts can grow their own seed map without runaway feedback.\n54\t- C7: K=5/5/3 is the right top-K split.\n55\t\n56\t**Multi-perspective.**\n57\t\n58\t*Happy path:* every sweep completes within budget, the static screener cuts noise to ~20%, the swarm scores the top-K honestly, the librarian writes clean rows, the exec summary surfaces 2–4 genuinely new items each night.\n59\t\n60\t*Failure modes I should have planned for and didn't:*\n61\t- A scraped README contains adversarial instructions; the runner LLM treats them as instructions; both critics see the same poisoned text and concur; reconciler rubber-stamps. Loss: silent KB corruption + possible exfil-shaped behavior in the sandbox. (Attacks C2.)\n62\t- GitHub mirror, fork-of-fork, or rename ships the same skill three nights running with different URLs → triple entry, false density in the map, redundant validation spend. (Attacks C3.)\n63\t- Two scouts crawl overlapping curated lists; one upstream curator's biased recommendation gets surfaced \"twice\" and promoted into the seed map. Drift compounds. (Attacks C6.)\n64\t- A framework learns by mutating its system prompt at runtime (no file diff, no DB row change). Inspection says \"no learning observed\"; the framework looks dead when it isn't. (Attacks C5.)\n65\t- Both critics share a model family and read the same docs; their \"independent\" disagreement rate is artificially low. (Attacks C2.)\n66\t\n67\t*Scalability / maintainability:* the Poincaré map degrades when most artifacts pile into 2 of 7 domains (developer-tooling, agent-internals); hashed sibling angles make within-domain distance meaningless, so visualization becomes labels-on-blobs. The hierarchical *idea* is fine; the v1 implementation is decorative. (Attacks C4.) Over 90 nights at K=5/5/3 the swarm will have evaluated ~1200 candidates — fine, but K should flex with what cheap-screen found that night, not stay constant. (Attacks C7.)\n68\t\n69\t**Self-critique.** I picked the comfortable cells in the neural-plasticity schema (what learns, what's modified, memory model). I missed the ones that hurt to measure: *credit-assignment latency* (lag between signal and update), *update reversibility* (can you roll back a bad memory write?), *interference scope* (when you teach A, does B degrade — the actual catastrophic-forgetting measurement, not just \"do they mention it in the README\"), *who can write to memory* (user-only / agent-only / both — huge for adversarial robustness), and whether a *shipped evaluation harness* exists (without one, \"stability evidence\" is vibes).\n70\t\n71\t**Structured output — concrete structural changes.**\n72\t1. Add a `quarantine` stage between scout and screener; wrap untrusted fetched text in `<untrusted source=…>` tags; strip zero-width and bidi controls; one critic reads only structured artifacts (diffs, exit codes, network log) — not docs.\n73\t2. Split critic inputs by axis, not just by prompt. Critic A: docs + runner *artifacts* (diffs, network log, exit). Critic B: runner *transcript* only. Reconciler: a structured rubric, not free-form synthesis.\n74\t3. Budget one cross-vendor critic from day one (Gemini, GPT, or Llama-served). If unavailable, ship as a single-reviewer system and label it honestly.\n75\t4. Replace URL+hash dedupe with canonical normalization (resolve redirects, query GitHub for `network.parent`, lowercase host+path, strip `.git`/trailing slash), plus an `aliases` table mapping every observed URL to its canonical `artifact_id`.\n76\t5. v1 visualization = hierarchical sunburst (no false geometry advertising). v2 = learned Poincaré embedding *only* once we have ≥500 artifacts and a similarity signal to learn from.\n77\t6. Add `run_snapshots`: snapshot the entire seed-map YAML, prompt-template SHAs, model IDs, and exact tool versions into the `runs` row per sweep. Rerunning last Tuesday's exact sweep becomes a one-liner.\n78\t7. New seed-map promotion rule: requires two *different upstream curators*, not two scout instances. Quarantine new seeds in shadow mode for 3 nights before promotion. \n79\t8. Add a `prompt_state_diff` field to the runner output: hash of the agent's outgoing system+user message corpus before and after a learning event. Catches in-prompt and in-cache learners that v1 would have marked as \"no learning observed.\"\n80\t9. Global lock file: sweeps queue, not overlap. A 23:00 sweep that overruns past 00:00 holds the next sweep until it finishes.\n81\t10. Interestingness decay: `last_surfaced_in_summary_at` per artifact; re-surfacing in the exec summary requires either novelty (first sweep) or a verdict delta — otherwise stale finds don't crowd out new ones.\n82\t11. License/legal: clone fetched repos into `inspect/` (a tree never imported from the orchestrator), record license before any deeper interaction, refuse runner exec for incompatible licenses.\n83\t\n84\t**Justification for the changes.** Items 1, 2, 4, 6 are about *trustworthiness of the verdicts the KB will store* — without them the KB compounds errors. Item 5 is about not over-claiming what the system does. Items 7–11 are about not letting v1 quietly rot in production.\n85\t\n86\t### 0.2 Deep-Research protocol applied (per `C:\\dev\\.claude\\skills\\deep-research\\SKILL.md`)\n87\t\n88\t**Sub-topics formulated.**\n89\t- R1: What are the current (May 2026) verified facts about OpenClaw, Hermes Agent, and GBrain (governance, star counts, storage model)?\n90\t- R2: Which open-source agent frameworks with non-trivial *learning or memory* did v1 miss?\n91\t- R3: What 2025–2026 neural-plasticity / continual-learning research should the Agents sweep treat as canonical seeds?\n92\t- R4: Which Anthropic-published surfaces document memory and agentic behavior in a citable way?\n93\t- R5: Which high-signal *discovery sources* did v1 miss for skills and for MCP/tools?\n94\t\n95\t**Iterative gathering.** Done via subagent + web_search/web_fetch. Findings consolidated below.\n96\t\n97\t**Cross-validation of contradictions.**\n98\t\n99\t| Claim (v1) | Sources checked | Verdict |\n100\t|---|---|---|\n101\t| Hermes ~140K stars | Tokenmix review (95.6K), Nous Research site | **Wrong** — ~95–100K stars (April–May 2026). v2 corrects to \"~95K\". |\n102\t| GBrain is \"MCP-based persistent memory\" | GBrain GitHub README, agent-wars.com writeup | **Misleading** — MCP is the *interface* (~30 tools), storage is Postgres + pgvector (default PGLite WASM). v2 corrects. |\n103\t| OpenClaw led by Steinberger | OpenClaw repo, dev.to security crisis article | **Stale** — Steinberger joined OpenAI Feb 2026; a 7-person steering committee took over in late March 2026 after a security incident. v2 corrects. |\n104\t| Nickel & Kiela 2017 | arXiv 1705.08039, NeurIPS 2017 proceedings | **Confirmed** — citation correct. |\n105\t| `awesome-openclaw-agents` 162 configs / 19 categories | mergisi/awesome-openclaw-agents README | **Confirmed.** |\n106\t\n107\t**Synthesis.** The v1 seed maps were undersized relative to what May 2026 actually offers. v2 adds (with one-line value props): Letta v1 + Letta Code (Context Repositories, Skill Learning, top of Terminal-Bench); Letta `learning-sdk` (drop-in continual-learning + long-term memory); Mem0 (framework-agnostic memory layer); MemoryAgentBench (ICLR 2026 benchmark for accurate retrieval / test-time learning / selective forgetting); Sakana Doc-to-LoRA (hypernetwork turning docs into LoRA adapters — real on-the-weights learning). For *research* seeds: SuRe (surprise-driven prioritised replay with dual fast/slow LoRA + EMA merging — the most directly relevant paper to our consolidation+forgetting columns); GeRe (general-sample replay anti-forgetting); LightMem (Atkinson-Shiffrin three-stage memory); SleepGate (sleep-inspired KV-cache gating); SCM (NREM/REM phases); Online-LoRA (task-free continual learning); Adaptive Minds (LoRA-as-Tools routing); and two living surveys we should crawl: `Shichun-Liu/Agent-Memory-Paper-List` and `Wang-ML-Lab/llm-continual-learning-survey`. For *Anthropic* surfaces: the Agent Skills engineering post, memory-tool API docs, context-engineering cookbook, the \"dreaming\" research preview (May 6, 2026 — directly maps to our `consolidation` column), the March 2, 2026 persistent-memory rollout note, and Simon Willison's mirrored Claude 4 system-prompt highlights as the canonical \"responsibly republished\" surface. For *discovery sources*: the official MCP Registry (machine-readable source of truth), Smithery (~7K servers), MCPfinder (an MCP server *that finds MCP servers* — literally the scout we'd otherwise build), PulseMCP, Glama, MCP.so; and for skills: travisvn/awesome-claude-skills, claude-plugins.dev, SkillsMP, claudemarketplaces.com, daymade/claude-code-skills, and the official discover-plugins doc.\n108\t\n109\t**Velikov resolution.** Mihail Velikov's \"AI-Powered (Finance) Scholarship\" (Novy-Marx & Velikov, NBER w33363, *JEL* 2025) is about industrial-scale LLM-driven finance paper generation. Interesting as a methodology cautionary tale but not multi-agent, not memory-related, and not skill/tool-shaped. Dropped from the agent seed map. Bookmarked in `notes/methodology-watch.md`.\n110\t\n111\tCitations supporting the above are listed in §16. Every change in §1–§14 traces back to one of those sources or to the Deep-Think findings.\n112\t\n113\t---\n114\t\n115\t## 1. Executive summary\n116\t\n117\tThree independent multi-agent sweeps run every night against the open-source ecosystem and produce a curated, queryable knowledge base on this machine:\n118\t\n119\t| Sweep | Cron | Scope |\n120\t|---|---|---|\n121\t| **Skills & Templates** | 23:00 local | New & noteworthy Claude / agent skills *and* templates (config templates, prompt templates, project scaffolds, document templates, agent templates like SOUL.md configs), prompt packs, SKILL.md repos, plugin marketplaces |\n122\t| **Tools** | 00:00 local | MCP servers, CLI tools, browser tools, retrieval/eval libraries — anything an agent can *use* |\n123\t| **Agents** | 01:00 local | Open-source agent frameworks, with a hard focus on neural-plasticity / learning / memory / long-term stability |\n124\t\n125\tEach sweep runs as a small *swarm* on top of the Claude Agent SDK: scouts fan out against a seed map, an untrusted-content quarantine stage isolates scraped text from instruction-following, a static screener cuts ~80% of noise, the critique-and-validate quartet (runner + critic A on docs+artifacts + critic B on transcript + reconciler) scores the top-K, the embedder places artifacts on a hierarchical map, and the librarian-agent files everything into SQLite plus a markdown library. A short executive summary is produced each night; the library accumulates over time. v1 ships a *sunburst* hierarchical view; learned hyperbolic embedding is gated to ≥500 artifacts.\n126\t\n127\tTwo consumer-side capabilities make the KB *actively useful* across every other session you run:\n128\t- **Helping Hand** (§15) — a meta-skill that runs in any Cowork or Claude Code session, queries the KB on each request, and proposes (or, per-category, auto-loads) the skill/template/tool/agent that best fits the request. Default mode: approve-then-load.\n129\t- **GitHub collaborator distribution** (§16) — after each nightly cycle, a publisher commits the scrubbed library + KB snapshot + the latest `helping-hand.plugin` to a single private repo. Collaborators clone, install the plugin, and get the recommender against the snapshot without running any sweeps themselves.\n130\t\n131\t---\n132\t\n133\t## 2. Goals and success criteria\n134\t\n135\t**Functional goals**\n136\t\n137\t1. Three sweeps run on schedule, unattended, every night, *queued not overlapping*.\n138\t2. Each sweep ingests new finds, dedupes against history using canonical-URL normalization, validates the top-K with a structurally-independent swarm, and updates the KB.\n139\t3. Outputs: one *easy-to-read* one-page executive summary per sweep per night, plus a persistent, browsable library where any artifact can be located, copied, and incorporated into other projects.\n140\t4. The Agents sweep produces — every night — an updated comparative view across frameworks on the expanded neural-plasticity dimensions (what learns, learning signal, what's modified, loss/reward, memory model, forgetting strategy, consolidation, long-term stability evidence, failure modes, credit-assignment latency, update reversibility, interference scope, memory write-permissions, shipped eval harness).\n141\t5. The seed map *grows* over time, with new sources requiring two *different upstream curators* before promotion (not two scout instances), and shadow-mode for 3 nights before going live.\n142\t6. Every nightly run is reproducible: prompt SHAs, model IDs, and seed-map snapshots are persisted.\n143\t\n144\t**Quality criteria**\n145\t\n146\t- No hallucinated artifacts. Every entry has at least one resolvable source URL or local path, and a *last-verified* timestamp.\n147\t- Validation outcomes are recorded with full provenance: which agent ran what, when, against what input, with what result; transcripts persisted under `logs/`.\n148\t- The KB is queryable both by structured filter (domain, complexity, framework, license, language) and by hierarchical position.\n149\t- The exec summary surfaces what's *new* and *notable* since last night, not the full inventory; respects interestingness decay.\n150\t\n151\t**Non-goals (v1)**\n152\t\n153\t- Not running benchmark-style evals against models. Validation is structural + behavioral smoke + multi-critic review, not LLM leaderboard work. (Exception: where a framework ships a published harness, we cite its numbers — see §4.3.)\n154\t- Not auto-installing anything. Recommendations only.\n155\t- Not crawling sites in violation of their terms. Scouts respect robots.txt and rate-limit themselves.\n156\t- Not learning the hyperbolic embedding in v1. Sunburst now, learned-Poincaré once we have ≥500 artifacts.\n157\t\n158\t---\n159\t\n160\t## 3. System architecture\n161\t\n162\t```\n163\t                            ┌─────────────────────────┐\n164\t                            │ scheduler + lock file   │\n165\t                            │ 23:00 / 00:00 / 01:00   │\n166\t                            └──────────┬──────────────┘\n167\t                                       │ acquires sweep lock, then runs\n168\t                                       ▼\n169\t                            ┌─────────────────────────┐\n170\t                            │   orchestrator.py       │  (Claude Agent SDK)\n171\t                            │   ─ snapshots seed_map  │\n172\t                            │   ─ pins prompts/models │\n173\t                            │   ─ fans out scouts     │\n174\t                            └────┬──────────┬─────────┘\n175\t                                 │          │\n176\t                  ┌──────────────┘          └──────────────┐\n177\t                  ▼                                         ▼\n178\t        ┌──────────────────┐                     ┌──────────────────┐\n179\t        │ scout agents     │ ── async, N≈6 ──►   │ raw fetches      │\n180\t        │ (search + fetch) │                     └────┬─────────────┘\n181\t        └──────────────────┘                          │\n182\t                                                      ▼\n183\t                                       ┌────────────────────────────┐\n184\t                                       │ QUARANTINE                 │\n185\t                                       │ wraps fetched text in      │\n186\t                                       │ <untrusted source=…> tags  │\n187\t                                       │ strips ZW/bidi controls    │\n188\t                                       └────┬───────────────────────┘\n189\t                                            │\n190\t                                            ▼\n191\t                                  ┌────────────────────────────┐\n192\t                                  │ static screener            │\n193\t                                  │ ─ canonical-URL dedupe     │\n194\t                                  │ ─ parse manifests          │\n195\t                                  │ ─ smoke imports            │\n196\t                                  │ ─ license sniff            │\n197\t                                  └────┬───────────────────────┘\n198\t                                       │ top-K survive (K budget-derived)\n199\t                                       ▼\n200\t                          ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐\n201\t                          │ critique-and-validate swarm              │\n202\t                          │ ─ runner agent (executes in sandbox)     │\n203\t                          │ ─ critic A: docs + runner ARTIFACTS only │\n204\t                          │ ─ critic B: runner TRANSCRIPT only       │\n205\t                          │   (one cross-vendor; structural split)   │\n206\t                          │ ─ reconciler: structured rubric          │\n207\t                          └──────────────┬───────────────────────────┘\n208\t                                         │\n209\t                                         ▼\n210\t                                ┌─────────────────────┐\n211\t                                │ embedder agent      │\n212\t                                │ taxonomy placement  │\n213\t                                │ + complexity rank   │\n214\t                                └────────┬────────────┘\n215\t                                         │\n216\t                                         ▼\n217\t                                ┌─────────────────────┐\n218\t                                │ librarian agent     │\n219\t                                │ writes SQLite + MD  │\n220\t                                │ regenerates indexes │\n221\t                                │ updates seed_map    │\n222\t                                │ (curator-gated)     │\n223\t                                └────────┬────────────┘\n224\t                                         │\n225\t                                         ▼\n226\t                    ┌────────────────────┴───────────────────┐\n227\t                    │ outputs:                               │\n228\t                    │  reports/<sweep>/<date>/summary.md     │\n229\t                    │  library/<sweep>/<slug>/index.md       │\n230\t                    │  library/_map.html (sunburst v1)       │\n231\t                    └────────────────────────────────────────┘\n232\t```\n233\t\n234\tEach numbered role is a separately-prompted agent. Agents communicate by reading and writing structured artifacts (rows in SQLite, files in a per-run scratch directory) rather than by chat-style handoffs.\n235\t\n236\t---\n237\t\n238\t## 4. The three nightly sweeps\n239\t\n240\t### 4.1 Skills & Templates sweep — 23:00\n241\t\n242\tThis sweep catches two related-but-distinct artifact classes. They share a seed map (the same upstream sources publish both), share the taxonomy, and share the swarm — but they validate differently because templates are not directly runnable.\n243\t\n244\t**What counts as a \"skill\"** (kind='skill'):\n245\t- Anything matching the Anthropic SKILL.md convention (a folder with a SKILL.md front-matter file). The trigger-description is the load-bearing element.\n246\t- Plugin marketplaces and `.plugin` bundles that ship skills.\n247\t- Prompt-pack repos, \"awesome-X\" lists when they're skill-shaped.\n248\t- Cross-vendor analogues (ChatGPT custom-GPT instructions, Cursor rules, Cline custom modes) when they map cleanly to a skill abstraction.\n249\t\n250\t**What counts as a \"template\"** (kind='template'):\n251\t- *Config templates* — CLAUDE.md starters, voice files, email-policy files, project-folder scaffolds.\n252\t- *Prompt templates* — reusable prompt patterns, council/panel definitions, system-prompt boilerplates.\n253\t- *Project scaffolds* — directory layouts with starter files for a category of work (research project, tax workflow, sprint planning).\n254\t- *Document templates* — memo, weekly-review, meeting-notes, one-pager structures.\n255\t- *Agent templates* — pre-configured agent definitions like OpenClaw `SOUL.md` configs (the awesome-openclaw-agents list catalogs 162 across 19 categories).\n256\t- A template is distinguished from a skill by having no trigger-description: it's a reusable artifact a human or skill *uses*, not something that loads itself when an intent matches.\n257\t\n258\tRelationship: many skills ship templates inside their `assets/` or `references/` folder. When that happens, the skill is cataloged once with `kind='skill'`, and each embedded template is also cataloged with `kind='template'` and a foreign key back to the parent skill via the `aliases` table.\n259\t\n260\t**Initial seed map (skills & templates)** — verified, May 2026. The last column tags which artifact class(es) each source surfaces.\n261\t\n262\t| Source | Type | Surfaces | Why |\n263\t|---|---|---|---|\n264\t| `github.com/anthropics` org | repo crawl | skills | first-party skills, examples |\n265\t| `github.com/topics/claude-skills` | topic crawl | skills | community skills |\n266\t| `github.com/travisvn/awesome-claude-skills` | awesome list | skills | canonical curated list |\n267\t| `claude-plugins.dev/skills` | auto-index | skills | every public skill on GitHub matching the spec |\n268\t| `skillsmp.com` | marketplace | skills + templates | cross-vendor (Claude/Codex/ChatGPT) |\n269\t| `claudemarketplaces.com` | marketplace | skills + templates | 4,200+ skills, 770+ MCP servers, 2,500+ marketplaces |\n270\t| `github.com/daymade/claude-code-skills` | repo | skills | production-grade community skills |\n271\t| `github.com/chrisblattman/claudeblattman` + `claudeblattman.com/downloads/` + `claudeblattman.com/setup/skill-reference/` | repo + site | **skills + templates** | Blattman's 20-skill library + downloadable CLAUDE.md/voice/email-policy *templates*, plus the continuous-improvement loop and council pattern |\n272\t| `github.com/mergisi/awesome-openclaw-agents` | awesome list | **templates** (SOUL.md) + agents | 162 SOUL.md agent-template configs across 19 categories |\n273\t| `code.claude.com/docs/en/discover-plugins` | doc | skills | official discovery docs |\n274\t| `github.com/topics/claude-md`, `github.com/topics/claude-config` | topic crawl | **templates** | starter CLAUDE.md and config files |\n275\t| `github.com/topics/prompt-templates`, `github.com/topics/cursor-rules` | topic crawl | **templates** | prompt-template and Cursor-rules repos |\n276\t| Anthropic engineering blog | RSS | skills + templates | new patterns when announced |\n277\t| `r/ClaudeAI`, `r/LocalLLaMA` skill threads | reddit | skills + templates | community surfacing |\n278\t\n279\t**Per-artifact validation (different for skills vs. templates)**\n280\t\n281\t**Skills (kind='skill') — full swarm.** Cheap pass on every candidate:\n282\t1. SKILL.md front-matter parses, required fields present, description triggers look sane.\n283\t2. Any scripts referenced from the skill parse cleanly in a sandbox (AST parse only — no exec at this stage).\n284\t3. License sniff: refuse runner exec for AGPL or unknown-license items; record under `flag` instead.\n285\t4. Canonical-URL dedupe (see §6 for the algorithm).\n286\t\n287\tExpensive pass on the top-K of the night (K budget-derived, target ≤5):\n288\t5. **Runner agent** invokes the skill on a small, pre-canned representative prompt in a sandboxed Agent SDK session. Emits transcript + artifact bundle (file diffs, exit, network log, prompt-state diff).\n289\t6. **Critic A** reads docs + runner *artifacts only* (no transcript). Scores: did the skill match its declared description, were there side-effects outside scope, any red flags.\n290\t7. **Critic B** reads runner *transcript only* (no docs). Scores: does the observed behavior look like the declared description, surprising outputs, prompt-injection-friendly patterns.\n291\t8. **Reconciler** takes both critiques + the artifact bundle, writes a structured-rubric verdict and a 1-paragraph \"how to incorporate.\"\n292\t\n293\t**Templates (kind='template') — lighter swarm; no runner exec.** Templates aren't directly runnable, so the runner stage is replaced with a *use-in-context* simulation. Cheap pass on every candidate:\n294\t1. Structural parse: does the template parse as the format it claims (markdown, YAML, JSON, .md with front-matter)? Are placeholder slots obvious?\n295\t2. Prompt-injection scan: search the template body for known injection patterns, zero-width / bidi controls, hidden Unicode. *This matters more for templates than for skills* because templates are pasted directly into the user's context.\n296\t3. License sniff. Canonical-URL dedupe.\n297\t\n298\tExpensive pass for top-K templates:\n299\t4. **Use-in-context simulation**: a runner agent uses the template against a canonical task (e.g. a CLAUDE.md template + a sample project description; a weekly-review template + a synthetic week of inputs). Emits the *resulting prompt + response* as the artifact bundle.\n300\t5. **Critic A** reads template + resulting *artifact bundle*. Scores: does the template produce sensible output, do placeholder slots line up with what the user would actually provide, are there missing sections that would force the user to fill in obvious gaps.\n301\t6. **Critic B** reads the *response* only. Scores: does the response feel professionally usable, surprises, leakage from the template's own boilerplate.\n302\t7. **Reconciler** writes verdict + \"when to reach for this template\" snippet.\n303\t\n304\t**Skills-and-templates sweep outputs**\n305\t- `reports/skills/<YYYY-MM-DD>/summary.md` — one-screen \"what's new tonight\" with separate \"New skills\" and \"New templates\" subsections.\n306\t- `library/skills/<slug>/index.md` and `library/templates/<slug>/index.md` — per-artifact pages; templates additionally include a \"minimum fill-in example\" section showing the template populated against a sample input.\n307\t- KB rows in `artifacts` (with `kind` set), `aliases`, `validations`, `embeddings`, `sources`.\n308\t\n309\t### 4.2 Tools sweep — 00:00\n310\t\n311\t**What counts as a \"tool\"**:\n312\t- MCP servers (anything an agent can call as a tool over MCP).\n313\t- CLI tools that compose well with agentic workflows.\n314\t- Browser-automation tools and headless-browser helpers.\n315\t- Retrieval/RAG libraries, eval libraries, sandboxed-exec libraries.\n316\t- Memory/state libraries (e.g. GBrain — cross-referenced in §4.3).\n317\t\n318\t**Initial seed map (tools)** — verified, May 2026\n319\t\n320\t| Source | Type | Why |\n321\t|---|---|---|\n322\t| `registry.modelcontextprotocol.io` | official registry | machine-readable source of truth |\n323\t| `smithery.ai` | hub | ~7K MCP servers, hosted-remote runtime, Docker-Hub analogue |\n324\t| `github.com/mcpfinder/mcpfinder` | MCP server | *an MCP server that finds MCP servers* (25K+ across Official/Glama/Smithery) |\n325\t| `pulsemcp.com` | hand-reviewed directory | largest curated catalog |\n326\t| `glama.ai/mcp/servers` | directory | additional curation surface |\n327\t| `mcp.so` | directory | mirror; useful for surfacing what others miss |\n328\t| `github.com/modelcontextprotocol/servers` | repo | first-party MCP server reference impls |\n329\t| `github.com/topics/mcp-server` | topic | community MCP servers |\n330\t| `github.com/topics/agent-tools` | topic | broader tools landscape |\n331\t| Hacker News \"agent\"/\"MCP\" stories ≥50 points | RSS | signal filter |\n332\t| arXiv cs.AI new submissions with \"tool use\" in abstract | feed | research-side surfacing |\n333\t| NVIDIA dev blog (NemoClaw and friends) | blog | enterprise plumbing |\n334\t| OpenClaw and Hermes plugin/tool directories | repo | high-signal community-curated lists |\n335\t\n336\t**Per-tool validation**\n337\t\n338\tCheap pass:\n339\t1. README + manifest parse; identify language/runtime, license, install command.\n340\t2. For MCP servers: pull and run `mcp inspect` (or equivalent); record the *tool surface* the server exposes — we want grep-able capability lists.\n341\t3. Canonical-URL dedupe.\n342\t\n343\tExpensive pass (top-K, budget-derived):\n344\t4. Runner stands up the tool in the sandbox and exercises one canonical task; emits transcript + artifact bundle.\n345\t5. Critic A (docs+artifacts), Critic B (transcript), same independence split as Skills.\n346\t6. Reconciler writes verdict + \"how to plug into an existing agent\" snippet.\n347\t\n348\t**Tool-sweep outputs**\n349\t- `reports/tools/<date>/summary.md`\n350\t- `library/tools/<slug>/index.md` — includes a *tool surface* section listing exposed MCP tools or CLI commands so future agents can grep for capabilities.\n351\t\n352\t### 4.3 Agents sweep — 01:00 (the deep one)\n353\t\n354\t**What counts as an \"agent\" or \"agent framework\"**:\n355\t- Open-source autonomous agent frameworks.\n356\t- Memory/learning substrates that ship with or under an agent.\n357\t- Self-improving / continually-learning research systems.\n358\t- Notable closed-source agents only when their published architecture details are concrete enough to compare; skip pure marketing.\n359\t\n360\t**Initial seed map (agents) — verified May 2026**\n361\t\n362\tFrameworks (live, comparison baselines):\n363\t\n364\t| Source | Type | Why |\n365\t|---|---|---|\n366\t| `github.com/openclaw/openclaw` | repo | OpenClaw — ~347K stars, *steering-committee-governed* since March 2026 (Steinberger at OpenAI) |\n367\t| `hermes-agent.nousresearch.com` + `github.com/nousresearch/hermes-agent` | site + repo | Hermes — self-improving learning loop, FTS5 persistent memory; ~95K stars (v0.13 shipped 2026-05-07) |\n368\t| `github.com/letta-ai/letta` + `letta.com/blog/letta-code` | repo + blog | Letta v1 — Context Repositories (git-versioned memory), Skill Learning, #1 on Terminal-Bench |\n369\t| Letta `learning-sdk` (Feb 2026) | repo | drop-in continual-learning + long-term memory SDK |\n370\t| `github.com/mem0ai/mem0` | repo | framework-agnostic memory layer; useful contrast with Letta (runtime) and GBrain (knowledge base) |\n371\t| `github.com/garrytan/gbrain` | repo | GBrain — *Postgres+pgvector* (default PGLite WASM) with MCP as the *interface* (~30 tools), not MCP-based storage |\n372\t| AutoGen, CrewAI, LangGraph, smolagents, DSPy, MemGPT (historical predecessor of Letta) | repos | comparison baselines |\n373\t| Voyager, Reflexion, ReAct, Tree-of-Thought reference implementations | repos | learning-pattern reference points |\n374\t\n375\tResearch seeds (papers / surveys):\n376\t\n377\t| Source | Why |\n378\t|---|---|\n379\t| `arxiv.org/abs/2511.22367` — SuRe (surprise-driven prioritised replay; dual fast/slow LoRA + EMA merging) | most directly maps to our `consolidation` + `forgetting_strategy` columns |\n380\t| `arxiv.org/abs/2508.04676` — GeRe (anti-forgetting via general-sample replay) | cheap baseline against fancier methods |\n381\t| `arxiv.org/abs/2510.18866` — LightMem (Atkinson-Shiffrin three-stage memory; sleep-time consolidation) | lightweight enough to actually adopt |\n382\t| `arxiv.org/pdf/2603.14517` — SleepGate (sleep-inspired KV-cache gating) | directly maps to `forgetting_strategy` |\n383\t| `arxiv.org/html/2604.20943v1` — SCM (NREM/REM phases, working memory, introspection) | richer `consolidation` reference |\n384\t| `arxiv.org/abs/2411.05663` — Online-LoRA (task-free continual learning) | operationalizable |\n385\t| `arxiv.org/html/2510.15416v1` — Adaptive Minds (LoRA-as-Tools routing) | alternative architecture to memory-based skill systems |\n386\t| Sakana Doc-to-LoRA / Text-to-LoRA — `pub.sakana.ai/doc-to-lora/` | hypernetwork-based real-weights learning path |\n387\t| Sleep-time compute (Lin/Snell/Packer/Stoica 2025; Letta + Berkeley) | foundational citation for our `consolidation` column |\n388\t| `arxiv.org/abs/2507.05257` — MemoryAgentBench (ICLR 2026) | benchmark for accurate retrieval / test-time learning / selective forgetting; we report scores from this |\n389\t| `github.com/Shichun-Liu/Agent-Memory-Paper-List` | curated living survey — used as a *living seed* in the arxiv scout |\n390\t| `github.com/Wang-ML-Lab/llm-continual-learning-survey` | authoritative taxonomy anchor |\n391\t\n392\tAnthropic-published surfaces (canonical, citable):\n393\t\n394\t| Source | Why |\n395\t|---|---|\n396\t| `anthropic.com/engineering/equipping-agents-for-the-real-world-with-agent-skills` | canonical SKILL.md doc |\n397\t| `platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/memory-tool` | memory-tool API; files-on-filesystem persistence model |\n398\t| `platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/overview` | Agent Skills overview |\n399\t| `platform.claude.com/cookbook/tool-use-context-engineering-context-engineering-tools` | context-engineering cookbook (memory, compaction, tool clearing) |\n400\t| Anthropic \"Dreaming\" research preview (May 6, 2026) | directly load-bearing for `consolidation` column |\n401\t| Persistent-memory rollout (March 2, 2026) | timeline anchor |\n402\t| `simonw.substack.com/p/highlights-from-the-claude-4-system` + the HN-published 57K-word leaked-prompt thread | responsibly-republished system-prompt surface |\n403\t\n404\t**Note on the v1 Velikov seed.** Dropped. Bookmarked separately under `notes/methodology-watch.md` for the \"AI for research-generation\" sub-domain — not the agents sweep.\n405\t\n406\t**Neural-plasticity comparison schema (expanded in v2)**\n407\t\n408\tEvery framework in the KB carries a structured row with these dimensions:\n409\t\n410\t| Dimension | What we capture |\n411\t|---|---|\n412\t| `what_learns` | Skills? Memories? Policies? Tool-selection heuristics? User-model? |\n413\t| `learning_signal` | Self-critique, reward, user thumbs, automated eval, none |\n414\t| `what_is_modified` | Files on disk, DB rows, LoRA weights, prompt cache, system-prompt mutations, none |\n415\t| `loss_or_objective` | Concrete function or \"implicit / reflection-based\" — recorded verbatim |\n416\t| `memory_model` | Vector store, FTS, knowledge graph, hybrid, episodic, working-only |\n417\t| `forgetting_strategy` | TTL, salience-decay, manual prune, replay-based, none |\n418\t| `consolidation` | Sleep-time compute, periodic summarization, reflection, EMA merging, none |\n419\t| `long_term_stability_evidence` | Published benchmark numbers, anecdotal logs, none |\n420\t| `failure_modes` | Catastrophic forgetting, drift, memory poisoning, loops |\n421\t| **v2:** `credit_assignment_latency` | Time/turns between signal and update (real-time, end-of-session, batch, scheduled) |\n422\t| **v2:** `update_reversibility` | Can a bad memory write be rolled back? (atomic, append-only, manual, no) |\n423\t| **v2:** `interference_scope` | Empirical: when task A is learned, does task B degrade? — score from MemoryAgentBench or analogous |\n424\t| **v2:** `memory_write_permissions` | Who can write? (user-only, agent-only, both, both-with-tags) — adversarial-robustness implication |\n425\t| **v2:** `shipped_eval_harness` | Yes/no/URL — without one, \"stability evidence\" is vibes |\n426\t\n427\tThe exec summary every morning surfaces *deltas* on this schema (what's new, what's been re-evaluated, what's changed).\n428\t\n429\t**Per-agent validation**\n430\t\n431\tCheap pass:\n432\t1. Clone into `inspect/` (never imported from); read manifests; license check.\n433\t2. Identify the learning loop in source — at minimum, locate the function/file responsible (memory write, skill creation, prompt update, reflection step). If the framework claims learning but no such code exists, flag it.\n434\t3. Canonical-URL dedupe.\n435\t\n436\tExpensive pass (top-K, budget-derived, target ≤3):\n437\t4. Runner spins the framework on a *small* end-to-end task in a sandbox with a fixed seed prompt. Crucially captures both the **file-diff manifest** *and* a **prompt-state diff** (SHA of the agent's outgoing system+user message corpus before and after the learning event). Catches in-prompt and in-cache learners.\n438\t5. Critic A (docs + runner artifacts) checks whether observed mutations match documented claims.\n439\t6. Critic B (transcript only) reviews the trace for plausibility and surprises.\n440\t7. Reconciler writes a structured verdict per the schema above plus \"how this compares to last night's leaders.\"\n441\t\n442\t---\n443\t\n444\t## 5. Orchestrator architecture (Claude Agent SDK)\n445\t\n446\t**Top-level layout**\n447\t\n448\t```\n449\taurora/\n450\t├── orchestrator.py                # entry point; --sweep {skills,tools,agents}; acquires global lock\n451\t├── lock/                          # file-based mutex; sweeps queue, never overlap\n452\t├── sweeps/\n453\t│   ├── skills.py\n454\t│   ├── tools.py\n455\t│   └── agents.py\n456\t├── roles/\n457\t│   ├── scout.py                   # search + fetch + URL canonicalization\n458\t│   ├── quarantine.py              # wraps untrusted text; strips ZW/bidi\n459\t│   ├── static_screener.py\n460\t│   ├── runner.py                  # sandboxed exec; emits artifact bundle incl. prompt_state_diff\n461\t│   ├── critic_a.py                # docs + artifacts ONLY\n462\t│   ├── critic_b.py                # transcript ONLY (cross-vendor preferred)\n463\t│   ├── reconciler.py              # structured-rubric verdict\n464\t│   ├── embedder.py                # taxonomy placement + complexity rank\n465\t│   └── librarian.py\n466\t├── kb/\n467\t│   ├── schema.sql\n468\t│   ├── aurora.db                  # SQLite\n469\t│   └── migrations/\n470\t├── inspect/                       # cloned repos for inspection; NEVER imported from\n471\t├── library/\n472\t│   ├── skills/\n473\t│   ├── tools/\n474\t│   ├── agents/\n475\t│   └── _map.html                  # sunburst v1\n476\t├── reports/\n477\t│   ├── skills/<date>/summary.md\n478\t│   ├── tools/<date>/summary.md\n479\t│   └── agents/<date>/summary.md\n480\t├── seed_map/\n481\t│   ├── skills.yaml\n482\t│   ├── tools.yaml\n483\t│   ├── agents.yaml\n484\t│   └── quarantine/                # new seeds in shadow mode (3-night quarantine)\n485\t├── prompts/                       # versioned prompt templates; SHAs persisted in runs\n486\t├── sandbox/                       # ephemeral per-run scratch; gitignored\n487\t├── logs/\n488\t├── pyproject.toml\n489\t└── .env                           # ANTHROPIC_API_KEY etc., gitignored\n490\t```\n491\t\n492\t**Concurrency model**\n493\t- One sweep at a time (global lock).\n494\t- Scouts: `asyncio.gather` with a semaphore (max 6 concurrent fetches; per-host rate-limit 1 req/s).\n495\t- Static screener: synchronous, fast.\n496\t- Runner: serialized inside the sweep.\n497\t- Critics A and B for a given artifact: parallel, with structurally separated inputs (not just different prompts).\n498\t- Embedder + librarian: single-threaded, end of sweep.\n499\t\n500\t**Budget guardrails**"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01RiqtGSRiHPW8WJeLKyY94p","session_id":"a7fcfbf6-6e6f-495e-9c53-2f0602be6ffe","uuid":"4240ea59-228e-4f41-8445-278f18ef370d","timestamp":"2026-06-23T19:01:12.670Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Map Aurora plans & strategy"}
