{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GHsUR1DWh1qe7WMLHz6uM5","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\"\"\"Migration runner for the Aurora KB.\n2\t\n3\t`_ensure_schema()` in orchestrator.py only runs the full `schema.sql` on a\n4\tvirgin DB (one with no `runs` table). It does NOT apply incremental changes to\n5\tan already-populated DB. This module closes that gap.\n6\t\n7\t`apply_migrations(conn)` reads `kb/migrations/*.sql` in lexical order and\n8\tapplies any whose target `schema_version` is not yet recorded. Each migration\n9\tfile must:\n10\t\n11\t  * be named `NNNN_<slug>.sql` (the numeric prefix orders application), and\n12\t  * end with `INSERT OR IGNORE INTO schema_version (version, applied_at)\n13\t    VALUES (<v>, datetime('now'));` so its target version becomes recorded.\n14\t\n15\tThe runner derives a migration's target version from the highest\n16\t`schema_version VALUES (<n>, ...)` literal in the file. A migration is skipped\n17\twhen that version already exists in `schema_version`. This makes the runner\n18\tidempotent: re-running applies nothing once every version is present.\n19\t\n20\tBoth `orchestrator.py` (via `from aurora.kb.migrate import apply_migrations`)\n21\tand the standalone aurora-kb MCP server (which is outside the `aurora` package\n22\tand imports this file by path) call this. Keep it dependency-free (stdlib only)\n23\tand side-effect-free at import time so the server can load it cheaply.\n24\t\"\"\"\n25\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n26\t\n27\timport re\n28\timport sqlite3\n29\tfrom pathlib import Path\n30\t\n31\t# Directory holding the *.sql migrations, resolved relative to this file\n32\t# (kb/migrate.py -> kb/migrations).\n33\tMIGRATIONS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent / \"migrations\"\n34\t\n35\t_VERSION_RE = re.compile(\n36\t    r\"schema_version\\s*\\([^)]*\\)\\s*VALUES\\s*\\(\\s*(\\d+)\", re.IGNORECASE\n37\t)\n38\t\n39\t\n40\tdef _ensure_version_table(conn: sqlite3.Connection) -> None:\n41\t    conn.execute(\n42\t        \"CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS schema_version (\"\n43\t        \" version INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, applied_at TEXT NOT NULL)\"\n44\t    )\n45\t\n46\t\n47\tdef _applied_versions(conn: sqlite3.Connection) -> set[int]:\n48\t    _ensure_version_table(conn)\n49\t    return {r[0] for r in conn.execute(\"SELECT version FROM schema_version\").fetchall()}\n50\t\n51\t\n52\tdef _target_version(sql_text: str) -> int | None:\n53\t    \"\"\"The highest schema_version literal in a migration file, or None if the\n54\t    file records no version (in which case it is always applied).\"\"\"\n55\t    versions = [int(m) for m in _VERSION_RE.findall(sql_text)]\n56\t    return max(versions) if versions else None\n57\t\n58\t\n59\tdef apply_migrations(conn: sqlite3.Connection, migrations_dir: Path | None = None) -> list[str]:\n60\t    \"\"\"Apply any pending migrations to `conn`. Returns the list of filenames\n61\t    applied (empty when the DB is already current). Idempotent.\n62\t\n63\t    A migration is applied when its target schema_version is not yet recorded\n64\t    (or when it records no version at all). Each file is run with\n65\t    `executescript` inside a single transaction; on success the migration's\n66\t    own `INSERT OR IGNORE INTO schema_version` records the version.\n67\t\n68\t    After all migrations apply, `_reconcile_add_columns()` runs a defensive\n69\t    second pass over every migration's `ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN` statement,\n70\t    re-applying each idempotently. This closes the rebuild-drops-later-columns\n71\t    hole (Wave C known issue D-fix, 2026-05-29): `0002_models.sql` rebuilds\n72\t    the `artifacts` table to widen its CHECK constraint and silently drops any\n73\t    column added by a later migration. Without the reconciliation pass, a\n74\t    fresh `schema.sql -> apply_migrations` build can produce a DB whose\n75\t    `schema_version` claims v19 but whose `artifacts` table lacks\n76\t    `security_status`. The reconciliation pass re-applies all\n77\t    `ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN` declarations after every migration round,\n78\t    tolerating \"duplicate column name\" exactly like the per-migration runner.\n79\t    \"\"\"\n80\t    mdir = Path(migrations_dir) if migrations_dir is not None else MIGRATIONS_DIR"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01ACwxJodXAE6No2Hnb7B6TY","session_id":"a7fcfbf6-6e6f-495e-9c53-2f0602be6ffe","uuid":"64cc775a-4581-40fa-86a1-17ceb3b2da32","timestamp":"2026-06-23T19:01:19.471Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Map Aurora architecture & code"}
